WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.510
In Marvel Comics, Legion is the incredibly
powerful, but often neglected, mutant son

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:09.140
of Professor Charles Xavier, who basically
has as many super-powers that writers want

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.349
to stick inside his nutty little brain.

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:15.860
Over the years, he's helped save the world,
but also nearly destroyed it a few times too.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.949
Now that the character has his own surreal
live-action series, it's time to dig deep

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:24.540
into the comic pages and explore the the untold
truth of Legion.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.720
Off the team

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.771
Superhero teams are filled with weirdos with
superpowers looking for redemption, and they

00:00:30.771 --> 00:00:33.430
don't get weirder than Marvel's various X-teams.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.300
One look and you'll see that they're stocked
with animal-themed berserkers with metal bones,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:43.050
a guy named Cyclops who has two eyes, and
an occasional creep with way, way too much

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.860
skin — whose superhero name is also Skin
— which is somehow a useful power.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.820
Legion has occasionally hung around with the
X-Men, and was almost a regular member of

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.250
X-Factor as well.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.160
But that plan fizzled out when writer Peter
David put his foot down.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.989
Even in a world where readers are meant to
accept superpowered characters running around

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:05.950
in skin-tight outfits, David felt nobody would
be able to accept that a character too unstable

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.509
to leave a mutant sanctuary island could serve
as a superhero.

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:14.130
Of course, mental stability isn't exactly
a trademark of most superheroes…

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:15.570
"I'm Batman!"

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.011
...but Legion was just too much to handle
in the early '90s.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:23.499
Of course, unstable superheroes would become
a trademark of later Marvel comics.

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:27.789
But with every known and unknown superhero
power lurking in his brain, why would Legion

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:29.130
need a team anyhow?

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.250
That's like saying Justin Timberlake really
needed N'SYNC.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.740
It's madness.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.039
Magneto stole his inheritance

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:40.359
Ask any mad supervillain and they'll tell
you that asteroid bases don't come cheap.

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:44.399
If you want to go big, you have to threaten
world governments, rob a whole lot of banks…

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:49.459
or just steal a pile of gold from a traumatized
Holocaust survivor, like in Uncanny X-Men

00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:50.779
#161.

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:55.520
The reason that arch-frenemies Professor X
and Magneto met in the first place is a lady

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.969
named Gabrielle Haller, who shows up when
Magneto is volunteering at an Israeli hospital.

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:05.100
Because the woman is abnormally traumatized,
they call in noted "miracle worker" Charles

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.950
Xavier to help her out.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.540
Xavier discovers her terrible past at the
hands of Nazis, who only kept her alive because

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.200
she knew the location of a major stash of
gold.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.660
Never ones to miss an opportunity, Baron Strucker
and HYDRA attack, intent on finding the gold,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.629
and Xavier and Magneto have their first real
team-up while trying to rescue Gabrielle.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:29.400
Like anyone on a long road trip, Magneto and
the Professor realize that their beliefs don't

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.530
really mesh, so Magneto ends up liberating
Haller's stash of gold.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:39.489
Xavier, meanwhile, ends up getting the girl…
which ultimately leads to Legion's birth.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:40.709
Destroy all humans

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:44.989
The Marvel Universe is full of people who
can read your thoughts, control your thoughts,

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:48.049
and just generally mess with your thoughts
on a hundred levels.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:52.780
The X-Men alone boast Professor X, Emma Frost,
Jean Grey, and Psylocke.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.019
This metric-ton of mind-powered mutants came
into play when the villain Shadow King took

00:02:57.019 --> 00:03:01.010
control of Legion, and revealed that he was
a threat to the entire world.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.640
Among his other weird abilities, the Shadow
King is able to feed off of negative energy.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:11.769
So, in Uncanny X-Men #278, he sets plans in
motion to hijack Legion's body and, along

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:16.349
with the captured mutant Polaris — who also
happens to be Magneto's daughter — manages

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:19.170
to increase fear and hatred across the entire
globe.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.500
Shadow King's manipulations revealed some
particularly troubling stuff about Legion's

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.500
power.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.320
Basically, he's always just a single thought
away from ending life as we know it, and he's

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.280
possibly the most powerful Omega-level mutant,
somewhere in the range of Mister Fantastic's

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.739
nerdy kid Franklin, and Billy Corgan.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:38.189
The Age of Apocalypse

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:41.879
You know that whole thing about stepping on
a butterfly in the past and changing the future

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:43.989
in terrible and unexpected ways?

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:50.370
"Oh, oh I wish, I wish I hadn't killed that
fish."

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.909
Apparently, Legion doesn't.

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:56.159
During one of his more lucid periods, Legion
realized that he was suffering from some pretty

00:03:56.159 --> 00:03:57.760
heavy abandonment issues.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.200
Rather than join a support group for people
who've been abandoned by their psychic dads,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.709
Legion decided that if Magneto hadn't always
been fighting his pop, then he would have

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:07.189
made more time for Legion.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:09.639
His solution was simple: kill Magneto.

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:14.800
In the pages of X-Men #41, Legion hopped into
the past and tried to kill Magneto.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.569
But Xavier ended up sacrificing himself to
save Magneto instead.

00:04:18.569 --> 00:04:23.620
Without the powerful Professor X creating
the X-Men to constantly thwart the evil mutant

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:27.750
Apocalypse, there was no one to stop the villain
from taking over America and ushering in the

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.120
creatively-named "Age of Apocalypse."

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.430
As for Legion, he became a living version
of the grandfather paradox, as he could now

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.770
no longer be born because of his time-traveling
shenanigans.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.690
Or until another writer wanted to use him
again.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:41.690
God killer

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:47.800
So, Legion can drive every human on Earth
insane, and time travel to usher in the literal

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.530
apocalypse, but topping the list of dangerous
things that Legion can do with his powers

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.780
is probably his ability to kill gods.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:59.280
In New Mutants #21, the mystical mutant Magik
needs to defeat the Elder Gods who tormented

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.020
her while she was stuck in Limbo, who have
just come through a portal and are threatening

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.260
to destroy Earth.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.840
Apparently, Magik learned a lot about devil's
bargains while she was trapped with demons,

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.530
so she summons Legion, specifically so he
can destroy gods… which he does with a single

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.530
thought.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.979
In her words, part of Legion is "the god-mutant,"
which is a pretty intense thing to put on

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:20.130
a business card.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:24.220
While it's frightening to think of Legion
as the face of any kind of divinity, his power

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.190
to destroy both gods and men is unquestionable.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.050
He's been erased

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:34.160
It's an open secret that death has lost any
real meaning in comic books, and it's always

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.640
just a matter of time and action figure sales
until writers and editors find some way to

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.539
bring dead heroes back to life.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:44.629
Like most super-people, Legion has died before
— multiple times, in fact.

00:05:44.629 --> 00:05:49.160
But what makes him different from other characters
is that he's actually been erased from existence.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.410
The first time happened during the Age of
Apocalypse storyline, which we've already

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:54.410
discussed.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.640
The next time Legion peaced out, it was by
choice.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.500
In X-Men Legacy #24, he looks at the damage
he's caused, and — worrying that he'd do

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.389
still more in the future — decides to erase
all traces of himself.

00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:09.940
Despite his ultimate power, remnants of Legion
remained behind for whatever writer wants

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.169
to tangle with them next.

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:13.720
He's biblical

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.360
One of the more provocative aspects of Legion
as a character is that his name is intentionally

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.669
derived from the Bible.

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.550
Specifically, a passage about a man possessed
by demons who runs into Jesus, who asks the

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.069
possessed man his name.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:30.980
His response was quoted, appropriately enough,
by a demon-dude named Blackheart in Marvel's

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.340
first Ghost Rider movie:

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:41.580
The bible's "Legion" is chock full of demons,
too strong to be held by chains, and kept

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.850
in isolation, so Marvel's version isn't too
far off.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:49.190
The biblical Legion has a happy ending when
Jesus is able to exorcise the demons out of

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:54.189
his body and into a herd of 2,000 pigs, who
quickly drown themselves, which PETA would

00:06:54.189 --> 00:06:56.139
probably have some huge problem with.

00:06:56.139 --> 00:07:00.509
Conversely, it seems that the comic book version
of Legion can never be fully cleansed of his

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:04.190
personalities, and must suffer with his demons
every day.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.750
It's possible that his biblical name points
to an eventual redemption, but until then,

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:11.939
the character will remain trapped in his fascinating
personal hell.

00:07:11.939 --> 00:07:15.169
Bad for Legion — great for fans of surreal
comic book television.

00:07:15.169 --> 00:07:16.610
Thanks for watching!

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.250
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.540
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

